I say — Know that the rights of kinsfolk and the womb-relationship are among the most emphatic obligations of the religion, for the Messenger of God (peace and blessings be upon him) said: God the Exalted says: "I am al-Raḥmān (the Merciful), and this is the raḥim (womb/kinship). I have derived for it a name from My Name. Whoever joins it, I join him; and whoever severs it, I sever him." {«أنا الرحمن وهذه الرحم شققت لها اسما من اسمي فمن وصلها وصلته ومن قطعها بتته»}

And he (peace and blessings be upon him) said: "Whoever is pleased that his term be extended for him and that his provision be expanded for him, let him join his kinship." {«من سره أن ينسأ له في أثره ويوسع عليه في رزقه فليصل رحمه»}

In another narration: "Whoever is pleased that his life be prolonged for him and his provision be widened, let him fear God and join his kinship." {«من سره أن يمد له في عمره ويوسع له في رزقه فليتق الله وليصل رحمه»}

And it was said to the Messenger of God (peace and blessings be upon him): "Which people are best?" He said: "The most God-fearing of them, and the most joining of their kinship, and the most commanding of good and forbidding of evil." {«أتقاهم لله وأوصلهم لرحمه وآمرهم بالمعروف وأنهاهم عن المنكر»}

And Abū Dharr (may God be pleased with him) said: "My intimate friend (peace and blessings be upon him) counseled me to join the kinship even if it turns away from me, and he commanded me to speak the truth even if it be bitter." {«أوصاني خليلي عليه السلام بصلة الرحم وإن أدبرت وأمرني أن أقول الحق وإن كان مرا»}

And he (peace and blessings be upon him) said: "Indeed, the raḥim is suspended from the Throne. And the joiner is not the one who reciprocates (al-mukāfiʾ), but the joiner is the one who, when his kinship is severed, joins it." {«إن الرحم معلقة بالعرش وليس الواصل بالمكافئ ولكن الواصل الذي إذا انقطعت رحمه وصلها»}

And he (peace and blessings be upon him) said: "Indeed, the swiftest act of obedience in reward is joining the kinship, such that a household may be wicked, yet their wealth grows and their number multiplies when they join their kin." {«إن أعجل الطاعة ثوابا صلة الرحم حتى إن أهل البيت ليكونون فجارا فتنموا أموالهم ويكثر عددهم إذا وصلوا أرحامهم»}

And Zayd ibn Aslam said: When the Messenger of God (peace and blessings be upon him) set out to Mecca, a man presented himself to him and said: "If you desire the white-skinned women and the dark-skinned she-camels, then you should go to the Banū Mudlij." The Messenger of God (peace and blessings be upon him) said: "Indeed, God has prevented me from the Banū Mudlij by my joining of the kinship." {«إن الله قد منعني من بني مدلج بصلتهم الرحم»}

And Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr (may God be pleased with them both) said: "My mother came to me, and I said: O Messenger of God, my mother has come to me, and she is a polytheist — shall I join her?" He said: "Yes." {«نعم»} In another narration: "Shall I give to her?" He said: "Yes, join her." {«صليها»}

And he (peace and blessings be upon him) said: "Charity upon the poor is charity, and upon the kinsman is two (rewards): charity and joining of kinship." {«الصدقة على المساكين صدقة وعلى ذي الرحم ثنتان»}

And when Abū Ṭalḥah wished to give in charity a garden that he owned and loved, acting upon the saying of God the Exalted: {«لن تنالوا البر حتى تنفقوا مما تحبون»} ("You will not attain righteousness until you spend from what you love"), he said: "O Messenger of God, it is for the sake of God and for the poor and the destitute." The Messenger of God (peace and blessings be upon him) said: "Your reward is incumbent upon God — divide it among your relatives." {«وجب أجرك على الله قسمه في أقاربك»}

And he (peace and blessings be upon him) said: "The best charity is upon the kinsman who is hostile (al-kāshiḥ)." {«أفضل الصدقة على ذي الرحم الكاشح»}

And this is in the meaning of his saying: "The best of virtues is that you join the one who severs you, give to the one who deprives you, and pardon the one who wrongs you." {«أفضل الفضائل أن تصل من قطعك وتعطي من حرمك وتصفح عمن ظلمك»}

And it is narrated that ʿUmar (may God be pleased with him) wrote to his governors: "Command the relatives to visit one another and not to dwell in close proximity (as neighbors)." He said this only because close dwelling engenders crowding over rights, and perhaps it engenders estrangement and the severing of kinship.

### |||| The Rights of Parents and Children

Know that the rights of the marriage bond (milk al-nikāḥ) have already been expounded in the Book on the Etiquette of Marriage. As for the bond of the right hand (milk al-yamīn), it likewise entails rights in daily companionship that must be observed. Among the last counsels of the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, was his saying: "Fear God regarding what your right hands possess. Feed them from what you eat, clothe them from what you wear, and do not burden them with work they cannot bear. What you love, keep; what you dislike, sell. And do not torment God's creation, for God has given you dominion over them — had He willed, He would have given them dominion over you." {«اتقوا الله فيما ملكت أيمانكم أطعموهم مما تأكلون واكسوهم مما تلبسون ولا تكلفوهم من العمل ما لا يطيقون فما أحببتم فأمسكوا وما كرهتم فبيعوا ولا تعذبوا خلق الله فإن الله ملككم إياهم ولو شاء لملكهم إياكم»}

He also said, peace and blessings be upon him: "The slave has his food and clothing in kindness, and he is not burdened with work he cannot bear." {«للمملوك طعامه وكسوته بالمعروف ولا يكلف من العمل ما لا يطيق»}

And he said, peace and blessings be upon him: "None shall enter Paradise who is deceitful, arrogant, treacherous, or evil in his treatment of slaves." {«لا يدخل الجنة خب ولا متكبر ولا خائن ولا سيئ الملكة»}

ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, may God be pleased with them both, related: A man came to the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, and said: "O Messenger of God, how often shall we forgive the servant?" The Messenger of God remained silent. Then he said: "Forgive him seventy times each day." {«اعف عنه في كل يوم سبعين مرة»}

ʿUmar, may God be pleased with him, used to go to the outskirts (al-ʿAwālī) every Saturday, and whenever he found a slave engaged in work beyond his capacity, he would lighten it for him.

It is related of Abū Hurayrah, may God be pleased with him, that he saw a man riding his mount while his slave ran behind him. He said to him: "O servant of God, carry him behind you, for he is your brother — his soul is like your soul." So he carried him. Then he said: "The slave continues to increase in distance from God as long as he walks behind his master."

A slave-girl said to Abū al-Dardāʾ: "I poisoned you a year ago, but it had no effect on you." He asked: "Why did you do that?" She replied: "I sought rest from you." He said: "Go, you are free for the sake of God."

Al-Zuhrī said: "Whenever you say to a slave, 'May God disgrace you,' he becomes free."

It was said to al-Aḥnaf ibn Qays: "From whom did you learn forbearance (ḥilm)?" He said: "From Qays ibn ʿĀṣim." It was asked: "What was the extent of his forbearance?" He said: "While he was sitting in his house, his servant-girl came to him with a skewer of roasted meat. The skewer fell from her hand onto one of his sons, wounding him fatally, and the boy died. The girl was stunned with terror. He said: 'Nothing will calm this girl's terror except emancipation.' So he said to her: 'You are free; no harm shall befall you.'"

ʿAwn ibn ʿAbd Allāh, when his slave disobeyed him, would say: "How similar you are to your Master! Your Master is disobeyed by His servants, and you disobey your master." One day the slave angered him, so he said: "You only want me to strike you. Go — you are free."

A guest was with Maymūn ibn Mihrān, and he hurried his slave-girl to bring dinner. She came quickly with a full bowl, stumbled, and spilled it over the head of her master Maymūn. He said: "O slave-girl, you have burned me!" She said: "O teacher of goodness and instructor of the people, return to what God Almighty has said." He asked: "What has God Almighty said?" She said: "He said: {«والكاظمين الغيظ»} — 'And those who restrain their anger.'" He said: "I have restrained my anger." She said: "And {«والعافين عن الناس»} — 'And those who pardon the people.'" He said: "I have pardoned you." She said: "Add more, for God Almighty says: {«والله يحب المحسنين»} — 'And God loves those who do good.'" He said: "You are free for the sake of God Almighty."

Ibn al-Munkadir related that a man from the Companions of the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, struck his slave, and the slave kept saying: "I ask you by God, I ask you by the Face of God!" — but he did not release him. The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, heard the slave's cries and went to him. When the slave saw the Messenger of God, he withdrew his hand. The Messenger of God said: "He asked you by the Face of God, and you did not release him. When you saw me, you withdrew your hand." The man said: "He is free for the sake of God, O Messenger of God." He said: "Had you not done so, the Fire would have scorched your face." {«لو لم تفعل لسفعت وجهك النار»}

He also said, peace and blessings be upon him: "When a slave is sincere to his master and worships God well, he has his reward twice." {«العبد إذا نصح لسيده وأحسن عبادة الله فله أجره مرتين»}

When Abū Rāfiʿ was emancipated, he wept and said: "I had two rewards, and one of them has gone."

The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: "The first three to enter Paradise and the first three to enter the Fire were shown to me. The first three to enter Paradise are: the martyr, a slave who worships his Lord well and is sincere to his master, and a chaste man of modest means with dependents. The first three to enter the Fire are: a tyrannical ruler, a wealthy man who does not give God's due, and a poor man who is boastful." {«عرض علي أول ثلاثة يدخلون الجنة وأول ثلاثة يدخلون النار...»}

Abū Masʿūd al-Anṣārī related: "While I was striking my slave, I heard a voice from behind me: 'Know, O Abū Masʿūd' — twice. I turned around, and there was the Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him. I threw the whip from my hand. He said: 'By God, God has greater power over you than you have over this one.'" {«والله لله أقدر عليك منك على هذا»}

He said, peace and blessings be upon him: "When one of you buys a servant, let the first thing he feeds him be something sweet, for it is more pleasing to his soul." {«إذا ابتاع أحدكم الخادم فليكن أول شيء يطعمه الحلو فإنه أطيب لنفسه»} — narrated by Muʿādh.

Abū Hurayrah, may God be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of God, peace and blessings be upon him, said: "When one of you is brought his food by his servant, let him seat him and eat with him. If he does not, let him hand him a morsel." {«إذا أتى أحدكم خادمه بطعامه فليجلسه وليأكل معه فإن لم يفعل فليناوله لقمة»}

In another narration: "When one of you has his slave suffice him the labor of preparing his food, having borne its heat and toil, let him seat him and eat with him. If he does not, let him hand him a morsel, or take a bite and turn it — gesturing with his hand — and place it in his hand, saying: 'Eat this.'"

Salman was visited by a man while he was kneading dough. The man said: "O Abū ʿAbd Allāh, what is this?" He replied: "We sent the servant on an errand, and we disliked burdening him with two tasks at once."

He said, peace and blessings be upon him: "Whoever has a slave-girl, protects her, treats her well, then emancipates her and marries her — for him are two rewards." {«من كانت عنده جارية فصانها وأحسن إليها ثم أعتقها وتزوجها فذلك له أجران»}

And he said, peace and blessings be upon him: "Each of you is a shepherd, and each of you is responsible for his flock." {«كلكم راع وكلكم مسئول عن رعيته»}

The sum of the slave's right is that the master shares with him his food and clothing, does not burden him beyond his capacity, does not look upon him with an eye of pride and contempt, pardons his slips, and reflects — when angered by his error or offense — upon his own disobedience and offenses against the rights of God Almighty, and his own shortcomings in obedience to Him, while God's power over him is greater than his power over his slave.

Fuḍālah ibn ʿUbayd related that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: "Three are not asked about: a man who abandons the community, a man who disobeys his leader and dies in disobedience — these two are not asked about — and a woman whose husband is absent, having sufficed her the burdens of the world, and she displays herself after him — she is not asked about. And three are not asked about: a man who contends with God over His cloak — and His cloak is pride, and His garment is might — a man in doubt about God, and a man in despair of God's mercy." {«ثلاثة لا يسأل عنهم...»}

Here ends the Book of the Etiquette of Companionship and Daily Conduct with All Classes of Creation.

I say: Lying is among the most abominable of sins and the most heinous of defects. Ismāʿīl ibn Wāsiṭ said: I heard Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may God be pleased with him, delivering a sermon after the death of the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, and he said: "The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, stood among us in this very place of mine a year ago." Then he wept and said: "Beware of lying, for it is with wickedness (fujūr), and the two of them are in the Fire." {«إياكم والكذب فإنه مع الفجور وهما في النار»}

Abū Umāmah said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: "Indeed, lying is one of the gates of hypocrisy." {«إن الكذب باب من أبواب النفاق»}

Hadith: "Great is the treachery that you tell your brother a hadith which he believes to be true from you, while you are lying to him." Recorded by al-Bukhārī in Kitāb al-Adab al-Mufrad and by Abū Dāwūd from the hadith of Sufyān ibn Usayd; Ibn ʿAdī declared it weak. Aḥmad and al-Ṭabarānī narrated it from the hadith of al-Nawwās ibn Samʿān with a sound chain.

Hadith of Ibn Masʿūd: "A servant continues to lie until he is written with God as a confirmed liar." Agreed upon (by al-Bukhārī and Muslim).

Hadith: "He passed by two men bartering a sheep and swearing oaths..." — in it he said: "One of them made it obligatory upon himself sin and expiation." Recorded by Abū al-Fatḥ al-Azdī in Kitāb al-Asmāʾ al-Mufradah from the hadith of Nāsikh al-Ḥaḍramī; and thus we have narrated it in the Amālī of Ibn Samʿūn. Nāsikh is mentioned by al-Bukhārī in this manner in al-Tārīkh, and Abū Ḥātim said he is ʿAbdullāh ibn Nāsikh.

Hadith: "Lying diminishes one's provision." Recorded by Abū al-Shaykh in Ṭabaqāt al-Aṣbahāniyyīn from the hadith of Abū Hurayrah; we have also narrated it in the Mashyakhah of al-Qāḍī Abū Bakr, and its chain is weak.

Hadith: "Indeed, merchants are the wicked..." — in it: "They speak and thereby lie." Recorded by Aḥmad and al-Ḥākim, who declared its chain sound, and by al-Bayhaqī from the hadith of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Shibl. He, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: "Three persons whom God will not speak to on the Day of Resurrection, nor look upon: the one who reminds others of his gift, the one who sells his wares by false oaths, and the one who drags his garment (in pride)."

He, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: "Whoever swears an oath by God and includes in it (something false) even the size of a mosquito's wing, it will be a black spot in his heart until the Day of Resurrection." {«من حلف حالف بالله فأدخل فيها مثل جناح بعوضة إلا كانت نكتة في قلبه إلى يوم القيامة»}

Hadith of Abū Dharr: "Three whom God loves..." — in it: "And three whom God detests: the merchant or seller who is excessive in swearing." Recorded by Aḥmad, and the wording is his; in it is Ibn al-Aḥmas, whose condition is unknown. He and al-Nasāʾī narrated it with another wording and a sound chain. Al-Nasāʾī also recorded from the hadith of Abū Hurayrah: "Four whom God hates: the seller who is excessive in swearing..." — and its chain is sound.

Hadith: "Woe to the one who speaks and lies to make people laugh — woe to him, woe to him." {«ويل للذي يحدث فيكذب ليضحك به القوم ويل له ويل له»} Recorded by Abū Dāwūd, al-Tirmidhī (who declared it good), and al-Nasāʾī in al-Kubrā from the narration of Bahz ibn Ḥakīm from his father from his grandfather.

He, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: "I saw as if a man came to me and said: 'Rise.' So I rose with him, and behold, there were two men — one standing and the other sitting. In the hand of the standing one was an iron hook, which he would thrust into the cheek of the sitting one and pull it until it reached the back of his neck; then he would pull it and thrust it into the other side, stretching it; and when he stretched it, the first side would return as it was. I said to the one who had roused me: 'What is this?' He said: 'This is a man who lied, and he is being punished in his grave until the Day of Resurrection.'"

From ʿAbdullāh ibn Jarād, he said: I asked the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, saying: "O Messenger of God, does the believer commit fornication?" He said: "That may occur." I said: "O Prophet of God, does the believer lie?" He said: {«لا»} — "No." Then he, may God bless him and grant him peace, followed it with the saying of God the Exalted: {«إنما يفتري الكذب الذين لا يؤمنون بآيات الله»} — "Only those who do not believe in the signs of God fabricate lies."

Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī said: I heard the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, supplicating and saying in his supplication: "O God, purify my heart from hypocrisy, my private parts from fornication, and my tongue from lying." {«اللهم طهر قلبي من النفاق وفرجي من الزنا ولساني من الكذب»}

He, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: "Three whom God will not speak to, nor look upon, nor purify, and for whom there is a painful torment: an elderly fornicator, a lying king, and a haughty pauper." {«ثلاثة لا يكلمهم الله ولا ينظر إليهم ولا يزكيهم ولهم عذاب أليم شيخ زان وملك كذاب وعائل مستكبر»}

Hadith of ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿĀmir: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, came to our house while I was a small boy. I went out to play, and my mother said: "O ʿAbdullāh, come, I will give you something." He said: "And what did you intend to give him?" She said: "Dates." He said: "If you do not do it, a lie will be recorded against you." Recorded by Abū Dāwūd, and in it is one not named. Al-Ḥākim said that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿĀmir was born during the lifetime of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, and did not hear from him. I say: It has a supporting witness from the hadith of Abū Hurayrah and Ibn Masʿūd, and their narrators are trustworthy, except that al-Zuhrī did not hear from Abū Hurayrah.

He, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: "If God were to grant me wealth equal in number to these pebbles, I would divide it among you, and you would not find me miserly, nor a liar, nor a coward." {«لو أفاء الله على نعما عدد هذا الحصى لقسمتها بينكم ثم لا تجدوني بخيلا ولا كذابا ولا جبانا»}

He, may God bless him and grant him peace, said — and he was reclining: "Shall I not inform you of the greatest of the major sins: associating partners with God and disobedience to parents?" Then he sat up and said: "And beware, false speech." {«ألا أنبئكم بأكبر الكبائر الإشراك بالله وعقوق الوالدين ثم قعد وقال إلا وقول الزور»}

Ibn ʿUmar said: The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: "Indeed, a servant may tell a lie, and the angel distances himself from him by a journey of a mile due to the stench of what he has brought." {«إن العبد ليكذب الكذبة ليتباعد الملك عنه مسيرة ميل من نتن ما جاء به»}

Anas said: The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: "Give me six things from yourselves, and I will guarantee Paradise for you." They said: "What are they?" He said: "When one of you speaks, let him not lie; when he promises, let him not break his promise; when he is entrusted, let him not betray; and lower your eyes, guard your private parts, and restrain your hands." {«تقبلوا إلي بست أتقبل لكم بالجنة فقالوا وما هن قال إذا حدث أحدكم فلا يكذب وإذا وعد فلا يخلف وإذا ائتمن فلا يخن وغضوا أبصاركم واحفظوا فروجكم وكفوا أيديكم»}

He, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: "Indeed, Satan has a kohl, a linctus, and a snuff. As for his linctus, it is lying; as for his snuff, it is anger; and as for his kohl, it is sleep." {«إن للشيطان كحلا ولعوقا ونشوقا أما لعوقه فالكذب وأما نشوقه فالغضب وأما كحله فالنوم»}

ʿUmar, may God be pleased with him, delivered a sermon one day and said: "The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, stood among us as I stand among you now, and said: 'Do good to my Companions, then those who follow them, then those who follow them.' Then lying will spread, until a man swears an oath without being asked to swear, and bears witness without being asked to bear witness." {«أحسنوا إلي أصحابي ثم الذين يلونهم ثم يفشو الكذب حتى يحلف الرجل على اليمين ولم يستحلف ويشهد ولم يستشهد»}

The Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: "Whoever narrates a hadith from me while he believes it to be a lie, then he is one of the liars." {«من حدث عني بحديث وهو يرى أنه كذب فهو أحد الكاذبين»}

He, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: "Whoever swears an oath involving sin in order to cut off thereby the property of a Muslim without right, he will meet God the Mighty and Majestic while He is angry with him." {«من حلف على يمين بإثم ليقتطع بها مال امرئ مسلم بغير حق لقي الله عز وجل وهو عليه غضبان»}

It is narrated from the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, that he rejected the testimony of a man for a single lie he had told. {«أنه رد شهادة رجل في كذبة كذبها»}

He, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: "Every trait the Muslim may be stamped or marked with (is permissible) except treachery and lying." {«كل خصلة يطبع أو يطوى عليها المسلم إلا الخيانة والكذب»}

Hadith: "There was nothing among the creation of God more severe in the eyes of the Companions of the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, than lying. Indeed, if a man from among the Companions was found to have lied, his heart would not be relieved until he knew that he had made repentance to God for it." Recorded by Aḥmad from the hadith of ʿĀʾishah, and its narrators are trustworthy, except that he said "from Ibn Abī Mulaykah or someone else." Abū al-Shaykh narrated it in al-Ṭabaqāt and said "Ibn Abī Mulaykah" without doubt, and it is sound.

Hadith: "Four traits — if they are in you, then what you have missed of the world will not harm you: truthfulness in speech..." Recorded by al-Ḥākim and al-Kharāʾiṭī in Makārim al-Akhlāq from the hadith of ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr; in it is Ibn Lahīʿah.

Hadith of Abū Bakr: "Adhere to truthfulness, for it is with righteousness (birr), and the two of them are in Paradise." Recorded by Ibn Mājah and al-Nasāʾī in al-Yawm wa al-Laylah; part of it has preceded at the beginning of this section.

Hadith of Muʿādh: "I advise you to fear God and to speak truthfully." Recorded by Abū Nuʿaym in al-Ḥilyah; it has preceded.

As for the reports (āthār): ʿAlī, may God be pleased with him, said: "The greatest of sins before God is the lying tongue, and the worst of regrets is the regret of the Day of Resurrection."

ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, may God have mercy on him, said: "I have not told a single lie since I tightened my waist-wrapper upon myself."

ʿUmar, may God be pleased with him, said: "The most beloved of you to us, before we see you, is the one with the best name; when we see you, the most beloved of you to us is the one with the best character; and when we test you, the most beloved of you to us is the most truthful in speech and the greatest in trustworthiness."

From Maymūn ibn Abī Shabīb, he said: I sat down to write a book, and I came upon a letter — if I wrote it, it would adorn the book, but I had lied. So I resolved to leave it, and I was called from the side of the house: {«يثبت الله الذين آمنوا بالقول الثابت في الحياة الدنيا وفي الآخرة»} — "God establishes those who believe with the firm word in the life of this world and in the Hereafter."

Al-Shaʿbī said: "I do not know which of the two is deeper in the Fire: the liar or the miser."

Ibn al-Sammāk said: "I do not think I am rewarded for abandoning lying, for I only abandon it out of disgust for it."

It was said to Khālid ibn Ṣabīḥ: "Is a man called a liar for a single lie?" He said: "Yes."

Mālik ibn Dīnār said: "I read in some of the books: 'There is no orator but that his sermon is presented against his deeds. If he is truthful, he is confirmed; and if he is a liar, his lips are clipped with scissors of fire — whenever they are clipped, they grow back.'"

Mālik ibn Dīnār said: "Truthfulness and lying wrestle in the heart until one of them expels the other."

ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz spoke with al-Walīd ibn ʿAbd al-Malik about something, and he said to him: "You have lied." ʿUmar said: "By God, I have not lied since I knew that lying disgraces its possessor."

Know that the objective of this chapter is not disclosed except through knowledge of the reality of love in itself, then knowledge of its conditions and causes, then reflection thereafter upon the verification of its meaning with respect to God—may He be exalted. So the first thing that ought to be ascertained is that love is not conceivable except after knowledge and perception, for a person loves only what he knows. For this reason, it is not conceivable that an inanimate object be described with love; rather, it is a property of the living, perceiving being. Then the perceived things, in their division, are divided into what accords with the nature of the perceiver, harmonizes with it, and delights it; into what opposes it, repels it, and pains it; and into what has no effect upon it by way of pain or delight. Thus, everything in whose perception there is pleasure and ease is beloved to the perceiver, and what in its perception there is pain is detested by the perceiver, and what is devoid of resulting in pain or pleasure is not described as beloved nor as disliked. So then, every pleasurable thing is beloved to the one who takes pleasure in it. The meaning of its being beloved is that there is in the nature an inclination toward it, and the meaning of its being detested is that there is in the nature an aversion from it. So love is an expression for the inclination of the nature toward the pleasurable thing. If that inclination is confirmed and strengthened, it is called passionate love (ʿishq). And hatred is an expression for the aversion of the nature from the painful, wearying thing; when it is strengthened, it is called abhorrence (maqt). This, then, is a principle concerning the reality of the meaning of love that must be known.

The second principle: Since love is consequent upon perception and knowledge, it necessarily divides according to the division of perceived things and the senses. For each sense there is a perception of a type of perceived things, and for each one of them there is pleasure in some perceived things, and due to that pleasure the nature has an inclination toward them, so they are beloved to the sound nature. Thus, the pleasure of the eye is in sight and the perception of beautiful visible things, comely, handsome, delightful forms; the pleasure of the ear is in pleasant, measured melodies; the pleasure of smell is in pleasant fragrances; the pleasure of taste is in flavors; and the pleasure of touch is in softness and smoothness. Since these perceived things by the senses are pleasurable, they are beloved—that is, the sound nature has an inclination toward them—until the Messenger of God—peace be upon him—said: "Made beloved to me from your world are three: perfume, women, and the coolness of my eye was placed in prayer." {«حُبِّبَ إِلَيَّ مِنْ دُنْيَاكُمْ ثَلاثٌ: الطِّيبُ وَالنِّسَاءُ وَجُعِلَتْ قُرَّةُ عَيْنِي فِي الصَّلاةِ»} So he called perfume beloved, and it is known that the eye and the ear have no share in it, but rather only the sense of smell. And he called women beloved, and there is no share in them except for sight and touch, not smell, taste, or hearing. And he called prayer the coolness of the eye and made it the most complete of beloved things. And it is known that the five senses do not attain it, but rather a sixth sense whose seat is the heart, which none perceives except one who has a heart. And in the pleasures of the five senses, beasts share with man. If, then, love were confined to the perceived things of the five senses—such that it be said that God—may He be exalted—is not perceived by the senses nor represented in the imagination, so He is not loved—then the special property of man and that by which he is distinguished from the sixth sense—which is expressed either as intellect (ʿaql), or light (nūr), or heart (qalb), or whatever expressions you wish, for there is no quibbling over terms—would be nullified. Far from it! For the inner vision (al-baṣīrah al-bāṭinah) is stronger than the outer sight (al-baṣar al-ẓāhir), and the heart perceives more intensely than the eye, and the beauty of meanings perceived by the intellect is greater than the beauty of visible forms to the eyes. So necessarily the pleasure of the heart in what it perceives of the noble, divine matters that are too exalted for the senses to perceive is more complete and more excellent, and the inclination of the sound nature and the correct intellect toward it is stronger. And there is no meaning of love except inclination toward what in its perception there is pleasure, as will come in its detail. So none denies the love of God—may He be exalted—except one whom deficiency has seated at the level of beasts, who has not transcended the perception of the senses at all.

The third principle: It is not hidden to man that he loves himself, nor is it hidden that he may love another for the sake of himself. But whether it is conceivable that he love another for its own essence, not for the sake of himself—this is something that may be obscure to the weak, until they suppose that it is not conceivable that a man love another for its own essence unless there returns to the lover from it a share other than the perception of its essence. But the truth is that this is conceivable and existent. So let us explain the causes of love and its divisions. Its explanation is that the first beloved of every living being is his own self and essence. The meaning of his love for himself is that in his nature there is an inclination toward the continuation of his existence and an aversion from his non-existence and destruction. For the beloved by nature is what is harmonious with the lover, and what thing is more perfectly harmonious than his own self and the continuation of his existence? And what thing is more greatly opposed and repugnant to him than his non-existence and destruction? Therefore, man loves the continuation of existence and hates death and killing—not merely for what he fears after death, nor merely out of caution against the agonies of death. Rather, even if he were snatched away without pain and made to die without reward or punishment, he would not be pleased with it and would be averse to it. And he does not love death and pure non-existence except due to enduring pain in life. And whenever he is afflicted with a trial, his beloved is the removal of the trial. If he loves non-existence, he does not love it because it is non-existence, but because in it is the removal of the trial. So destruction and non-existence are abhorrent, and the continuation of existence is beloved. And just as the continuation of existence is beloved, so too the perfection of existence is beloved. For the deficient one is lacking perfection, and deficiency is non-existence relative to the missing portion, and it is destruction with respect to him. And destruction and non-existence are abhorrent in attributes, and the perfection of existence is likewise abhorrent in the root of the essence. And the existence of the attributes of perfection is beloved, just as the continuation of the root of existence is beloved. And this is an innate disposition in natures by the decree of the sunnah of God—and you will not find in the sunnah of God any alteration. {«وَلَنْ تَجِدَ لِسُنَّةِ اللَّهِ تَبْدِيلًا»}

So then, the first beloved of man is his own self, then the soundness of his limbs, then his wealth, his offspring, his clan, and his friends. For the limbs are beloved and their soundness is sought because the perfection of existence and the continuation of existence are dependent upon them. And wealth is beloved because it too is an instrument in the continuation of existence and its perfection, and likewise all other causes. So man loves these things not for their own essences, but for the connection of his share in the continuation of existence and its perfection to them—until he loves his offspring even though no share reaches him from them, and he endures hardships for their sake, because they succeed him in existence after his non-existence. So in the survival of his progeny there is a kind of survival for him. And due to the excess of his love for the survival of his own self, he loves the survival of one who stands in his place, as if he were a part of him, when he is unable to hope for the survival of his own self forever. Indeed, if he were given the choice between his own death and the death of his offspring, and his nature remained upon its equilibrium, he would prefer the survival of his own self over the survival of his offspring, because the survival of his offspring resembles his survival in some respect, but it is not his verified survival. Likewise, his love for his relatives and clan reverts to his love for the perfection of his own self, for he sees himself as numerous through them, strong by means of them, adorned by their perfection. For the clan, wealth, and external causes are like a wing that completes man. And the perfection of existence and its continuation are beloved by nature necessarily. So then, the first beloved of every living being is his own essence, the perfection of his essence, and the continuation of all that; and the hated thing with him is the opposite of that. This, then, is the first cause.

The second cause: Beneficence (iḥsān). For man is a slave of beneficence, and hearts have been created with an innate disposition to love the one who does good to them and to hate the one who does evil to them. And the Messenger of God—peace be upon him—said: "O God, do not give a wicked person authority over me, such that my heart loves him." {«اللَّهُمَّ لا تَجْعَلْ لِفَاجِرٍ عَلَيَّ يَدًا فَيُحِبَّهُ قَلْبِي»}

The fourth principle, concerning the explanation of the meaning of beauty (ḥusn) and comeliness (jamāl): Know that one confined in the narrowness of imaginations and sensed things might suppose that there is no meaning of beauty and comeliness except the proportion of the constitution and form, the beauty of color, whiteness tinged with redness, the extension of stature, and other such things described of the beauty of a person's form. For the beauty most prevalent among creatures is the beauty of sight, and most of their attention is directed to the forms of persons. So they suppose that what is not visible, not imaginable, not shaped, and not colored is not estimable, so its beauty is not conceivable; and if its beauty is not conceivable, then there is no pleasure in its perception, so it is not beloved. But this is a manifest error. For beauty is not confined to the perceived things of sight, nor to the proportion of the constitution and the blending of whiteness with redness. For we say: "This is beautiful handwriting, this is a beautiful voice, this is a beautiful horse." Rather, we say: "This is a beautiful garment, this is a beautiful vessel." So what meaning is there for the beauty of a voice, handwriting, and all other things, if beauty were only in the form? And it is known that the eye delights in looking at beautiful handwriting, and the ear delights in hearing beautiful, pleasant melodies. And there is nothing among perceived things except that it is divided into beautiful and ugly. So what is the meaning of beauty in which these things share? There is no escape from investigating it. And this investigation would be lengthy, and it is not fitting for the science of practical religion (ʿilm al-muʿāmalah) to elaborate upon it. So let us state the truth plainly and say: Everything—its beauty and comeliness lie in the presence of its perfection (kamāl) that befits it and is possible for it. So if all its possible perfections are present, it is in the utmost beauty; and if only some are present, then it has of beauty and comeliness according to what is present. Thus, the beautiful horse is the one that has gathered everything befitting a horse of form, shape, color, beauty of running, and ease of attack and retreat. And beautiful handwriting is that which has gathered everything befitting handwriting of the proportion of letters, their parallelism, the straightness of their arrangement, and the beauty of their order. And for everything there is a perfection that befits it, and what befits one may be opposed to another. So the beauty of each thing lies in its perfection that befits it. Man is not beautified by what beautifies a horse, nor is handwriting beautified by what beautifies a voice, nor are vessels beautified by what beautifies garments, and likewise all other things.

If you say: "These things, even if not all of them are perceived by the sense of sight—such as sounds and tastes—yet they are not free from the perception of the senses for them, so they are sensed things. And beauty and comeliness are not denied for sensed things, nor is the occurrence of pleasure in perceiving their beauty denied. Rather, that is denied only for what is not perceived by the senses." Then know that beauty and comeliness exist in non-sensed things, for it is said: "This is a beautiful character (khuluq ḥasan), this is beautiful knowledge, this is a beautiful conduct (sīrah ḥasanah), these are beautiful morals (akhlāq jamīlah)." And by beautiful morals is meant knowledge, intellect, chastity, courage, piety, generosity, manliness, and the rest of the qualities of goodness. And none of these attributes is perceived by the five senses; rather, they are perceived by the light of inner vision (nūr al-baṣīrah al-bāṭinah). And all these beautiful qualities are beloved, and the one described with them is beloved by nature to whoever knows his attributes. And the proof that the matter is so is that natures are created with an innate disposition to love the prophets—God's blessings be upon them—and to love the Companions—may God be pleased with them—even though they did not see them. Rather, [they love] the masters of the schools of law (madhhāhib), such as al-Shāfiʿī, Abū Ḥanīfah, Mālik, and others—until a man's love for the master of his school may exceed the limit of passionate love (ʿishq), leading him to spend all his wealth in supporting his school and defending it, and to risk his soul in fighting against those who impugn his imam and leader. How much blood has been shed in support of the masters of the schools! Would that I knew—who loves al-Shāfiʿī, for example, and loves him without ever having seen his form? And had he seen him, perhaps he would not have deemed his form handsome. So his approval that drove him to excessive love was for his inner form (ṣūrah bāṭinah), not his outer form. For his outer form has turned to dust with the dust. Rather, he loves him for his inner attributes of religion, piety, abundance of knowledge, mastery of the avenues of religion, his rising to impart the knowledge of the Sacred Law, and his spreading these goods throughout the world. And these are beautiful matters whose beauty is perceived only by the light of inner vision. As for the senses, they fall short of them. Likewise, whoever loves Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq—may God be pleased with him—and prefers him over others, or loves ʿAlī—may God be pleased with him—and prefers him and is partisan for him, loves them only for the approval of their inner forms of knowledge, religion, piety, courage, generosity, and other such things. So it is known that whoever loves al-Ṣiddīq—may God be pleased with him—for example, does not love his bones, flesh, skin, limbs, and shape, for all that has passed away, changed, and become non-existent. But what remains is that by which al-Ṣiddīq was al-Ṣiddīq—namely, the praiseworthy attributes that are the sources of beautiful conduct. So love remains with the survival of those attributes, despite the passing of all forms. And those attributes, in their entirety, revert to knowledge (ʿilm) and power (qudrah)—when one knows the realities of things and is able to compel himself upon them by subduing his appetites. So all the qualities of goodness branch out from these two descriptions, and they are not perceived by the senses, and their locus in the totality of the body is an indivisible part (juzʾ lā yatajazzaʾ). So it is the truly beloved, and the indivisible part has no form, shape, or color that appears to the sight such that it would be beloved for its sake. So then, beauty exists in conduct. And if beautiful conduct issued from other than knowledge and insight, it would not necessitate love. So the beloved is the source of beautiful conduct—namely, praiseworthy morals and noble virtues—and they revert in their entirety to the perfection of knowledge and power, and that is beloved by nature and not perceived by the senses. Until even a child, left to his nature, if we wished to make him love an absent or present person, living or dead, we would have no means except by elaborating in describing him with courage, generosity, knowledge, and the rest of the praiseworthy qualities. So whenever he believes that, he cannot restrain himself and cannot but love him. Did the Companions—may God be pleased with them—prevail, and the hatred of Abū Jahl and of Iblīs—may God curse him—except through elaboration in describing the beauties and uglinesses that are not perceived by the senses? Rather, when people described Ḥātim with generosity and described Khālid with courage, hearts loved them with a necessary love. And that is not from looking at a sensed form, nor from a share the lover attains from them. Rather, when the justice, beneficence, and outpouring of good of some king in some region of the earth is narrated, his love prevails over hearts, despite despair of the spread of his beneficence to the lovers due to the distance of the place and the remoteness of the lands. So then, man's love is not confined to the one who has done good to him. Rather, the beneficent one in himself is beloved, even if his beneficence never reaches the lover. For every beauty and comeliness is beloved, and the form is both outer and inner, and beauty and comeliness encompass both. The outer forms are perceived by the outer sight, and the inner forms by the inner vision. So whoever is deprived of inner vision does not perceive them, does not delight in them, does not love them, and does not incline toward them. And whoever's inner [vision] prevails over his outer senses, his love for inner meanings is greater than his love for outer meanings. How far between the one who loves a painted image on a wall for the beauty of its outer form, and the one who loves a prophet among the prophets for the beauty of his inner form!

The fifth cause: The hidden affinity (al-munāsabah al-khafiyyah) between the lover and the beloved. For there may be two persons between whom love is strengthened not due to beauty or a share, but merely due to the harmony of spirits. As the Prophet—peace be upon him—said: "Those of them that recognize one another are in harmony, and those of them that are alien to one another are in discord." {«فَمَا تَعَارَفَ مِنْهَا ائْتَلَفَ وَمَا تَنَاكَرَ مِنْهَا اخْتَلَفَ»}

I say: The visitation of graves is commendable (mustaḥabb) in general, for the sake of remembrance (tadhakkur) and admonition (iʿtibār); and the visitation of the graves of the righteous is [commendable] for the sake of seeking blessing (tabarruk) along with admonition. The Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, had forbidden the visitation of graves, then he permitted it afterwards. It is related from ʿAlī, may God be pleased with him, from the Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, that he said: "I had forbidden you from visiting graves, so visit them, for they remind you of the Hereafter — but do not speak obscenity (hajr)." {«كنت نهيتكم عن زيارة القبور فزوروها فإنها تذكركم الآخرة غير أن لا تقولوا هجرا»}

The Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, visited the grave of his mother with a thousand veiled men, and no day saw more weeping than that day. On that day he said: "I have been granted permission for the visitation, but not for seeking forgiveness [for her]," as we have already mentioned previously.

Ibn Abī Mulaykah said: I came upon ʿĀʾishah, may God be pleased with her, one day returning from the graves, and I said: "O Mother of the Believers, from where have you come?" She said: "From the grave of my brother ʿAbd al-Raḥmān." I said: "Was it not the case that the Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, had forbidden this?" She said: "Yes, then he commanded it."

However, one should not seize upon this [permission] to grant women leave to go out to the graves, for they multiply obscene speech (hajr) at the gravesides; thus the good of their visitation does not outweigh its evil, and they are not free from uncovering themselves and displaying their adornment (tabarruj) on the road. These are grave offenses, and visitation is [merely] a sunnah — so how can such [harms] be tolerated for its sake?

Yes, there is no harm in a woman going out in humble garments (thiyāb badhlah) that repel men's glances from her, on the condition that she restrict herself to supplication and refrain from conversation at the graveside.

Abū Dharr said: The Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, said: "Visit the graves, for they remind you of the Hereafter; and wash the dead, for the handling of a hollowed body is an eloquent admonition; and pray over the funeral processions, perhaps that will sadden you, for the sorrowful one is in the shade of God." {«زر القبور تذكر بها الآخرة واغسل الموتى فإن معالجة جسد خاو موعظة بليغة وصل على الجنائز لعل ذلك أن يحزنك فإن الحزين في ظل الله»}

Ibn Abī Mulaykah said: The Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, said: "Visit your dead and salute them, for in them you have a lesson (ʿibrah)." {«زوروا موتاكم وسلموا عليهم فإن لكم فيهم عبرة»}

It is related from Nāfiʿ that Ibn ʿUmar would not pass by any grave except that he stopped at it and saluted it. And from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, that Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Prophet, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, used to visit the grave of her uncle Ḥamzah on certain days, and would pray and weep at it.

The Prophet, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, said: "Whoever visits the grave of his parents, or one of them, every Friday, is forgiven and is recorded as dutiful (barr)." {«من زار قبر والديه أو أحدهما في كل جمعة غفر له وكتب برا»}

It is related from Ibn Sīrīn that he said: The Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, said: "A man may die while being undutiful (ʿāqq) to his parents, then he supplicates God for them after their death, and God records him among the dutiful (al-bārrīn)." {«إن الرجل ليموت والداه وهو عاق لهما فيدعو الله لهما من بعدهما فيكتبه الله من البارين»}

The Prophet, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, said: "Whoever visits my grave, my intercession (shafāʿah) becomes obligatory for him." {«من زار قبري فقد وجبت له شفاعتي»}

And he, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, said: "Whoever visits me in Medina, seeking [God's] reward (muḥtasiban), I shall be an intercessor and witness for him on the Day of Resurrection." {«من زارني بالمدينة محتسبا كنت له شفيعا وشهيدا يوم القيامة»}

Kaʿb al-Aḥbār said: No dawn breaks except that seventy thousand angels descend until they surround the grave, beating their wings and sending blessings upon the Prophet, may God's peace and blessings be upon him; until when evening comes, they ascend, and the like of them descend and do the same — until when the earth is split open, he emerges amidst seventy thousand angels honoring him.

The commendable practice (mustaḥabb) in visiting graves is that one stand with his back to the qiblah, facing the deceased with his face, and that he salute — and that he not wipe the grave, nor touch it, nor kiss it, for that is the custom of the Christians. Nāfiʿ said: I saw Ibn ʿUmar, a hundred times or more, come to the grave and say: "Peace be upon the Prophet, peace be upon Abū Bakr, peace be upon my father" — then he would turn away.

Abū Umāmah said: I saw Anas ibn Mālik come to the grave of the Prophet, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, and stand, then raise his hands until I thought he had begun the prayer; he saluted the Prophet, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, then turned away.

ʿĀʾishah, may God be pleased with her, said: The Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, said: "No man visits the grave of his brother and sits beside him, except that [the deceased] is comforted by him and responds to him until he departs." {«ما من رجل يزور قبر أخيه ويجلس عنده إلا استأنس به ورد عليه حتى يقوم»}

Sulaymān ibn Suḥaym said: I saw the Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, in a dream, and I said: "O Messenger of God, these people who come to you and salute you — do you comprehend their salutations?" He said: "Yes, and I respond to them."

Abū Hurayrah said: When a man passes by the grave of a person he knows and salutes him, [the deceased] returns the salutation and recognizes him; and when he passes by a grave of one he does not know and salutes him, [the deceased] returns the salutation.

A man from the family of ʿĀṣim al-Jaḥdarī said: I saw ʿĀṣim in my dream two years after his death, and I said: "Did you not die?" He said: "Yes." I said: "Where are you?" He said: "By God, I am in a meadow (rawḍah) from the meadows of Paradise, I and a group of my companions; we gather every Thursday night and its morning with Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Muzanī, and we exchange news of you." I said: "Your bodies, or your souls?" He said: "Far be it! The bodies have decayed; it is only the souls that meet." I said: "Do you know of our visiting you?" He said: "Yes, we come to know of it on Thursday evening, and all of Friday, and Saturday until sunrise." I said: "How is that, and not all the days?" He said: "Due to the excellence and greatness of Friday."

Muḥammad ibn Wāsiʿ used to visit [the graves] on Friday, and it was said to him: "Would you delay it to Monday?" He said: "It has reached me that the dead know of their visitors on Friday, and the day before it, and the day after it."

Al-Ḍaḥḥāk said: Whoever visits a grave before sunrise on Saturday, the deceased comes to know of his visitation. It was said: "How is that?" He said: "Because of the standing of Friday."

Bishr ibn Manṣūr said: During the time of the plague, there was a man who used to frequent the cemetery (al-jabbānah), attending the funeral prayers. When evening came, he would stand at the gate of the graves and say: "May God comfort your loneliness (waḥshah), have mercy on your estrangement (ghurbah), overlook your evil deeds, and accept your good deeds" — adding nothing to these words. The man said: "One evening I returned to my family and did not come to the graves to supplicate as I used to. While I was sleeping, behold, a great multitude came to me. I said: 'Who are you, and what is your need?' They said: 'We are the people of the graves.' I said: 'What has brought you?' They said: 'You have accustomed us to a gift from you when you return to your family.' I said: 'What is it?' They said: 'The supplications you used to make for us.' I said: 'Then I shall return to that.' And I never abandoned it after that."

Bashshār ibn Ghālib al-Najrānī said: I saw Rābiʿah al-ʿAdawiyyah, the devotee, in my dream — and I had been frequent in supplication for her. She said to me: "O Bashshār ibn Ghālib, your gifts come to us upon a tray of light, veiled with handkerchiefs of silk." I said: "How is that?" She said: "Thus it is: when living believers supplicate for the dead and their supplication is answered, that supplication is placed upon trays of light and veiled with handkerchiefs of silk, then it is brought to the deceased, and it is said: 'This is the gift of so-and-so to you.'"

The Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, said: "The deceased in his grave is like a drowning man frantically seeking help, awaiting a supplication that reaches him from his father, or his brother, or a friend of his. When it reaches him, it is dearer to him than the world and all that is in it. And the gifts of the living to the dead are supplication and seeking forgiveness (istighfār)." {«ما الميت في قبره إلا كالغريق المتغوث ينتظر دعوة تلحقه من أبيه أو أخيه أو صديق له فإذا لحقته كان أحب إليه من الدنيا وما فيها وإن هدايا الأحياء للأموات الدعاء والاستغفار»}

The ḥadīth of Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Azdī: He said: I witnessed Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī while he was in the throes of death, and he said: "O Saʿīd, when I die, do with me as the Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, commanded us." He said: "When one of you dies and you have leveled the earth over him, let one of you stand at the head of his grave and say: 'O so-and-so, son of so-and-so...'" — the ḥadīth concerning the instruction (talqīn) of the deceased in his grave, transmitted by al-Ṭabarānī.

There is no harm in reciting the Qurʾān at the graves. It is related from ʿAlī ibn Mūsā al-Ḥaddād that he said: I was with Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal at a funeral, and Muḥammad ibn Qudāmah al-Jawharī was with us. When the deceased was buried, a blind man came and recited at the grave. Aḥmad said to him: "O this one, recitation at the grave is an innovation (bidʿah)." When we left the cemetery, Muḥammad ibn Qudāmah said to Aḥmad: "O Abū ʿAbd Allāh, what do you say about Mubashshir ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ḥalabī?" He said: "Trustworthy (thiqah)." He said: "Have you written anything from him?" He said: "Yes." He said: "Mubashshir ibn Ismāʿīl informed me, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Lajlāj, from his father, that he had bequeathed that when he is buried, the opening of Sūrat al-Baqarah and its closing be recited at his head — and he said: I heard Ibn ʿUmar bequeathing that." So Aḥmad said to him: "Return to the man and tell him to recite."

Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Marwazī said: I heard Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal say: "When you enter the cemeteries, recite the Opening of the Book (Fātiḥah), the two Muʿawwidhatān, and Qul Huwa Allāhu Aḥad, and assign the reward of that to the people of the graves, for it reaches them."

Abū Qilābah said: I came from Syria to Basra and alighted at al-Khandaq. I performed ablution and prayed two rakʿahs by night, then I laid my head upon a grave and slept. Then I awoke, and behold, the occupant of the grave was complaining of me, saying: "You have harmed me since last night." Then he said: "You do not know, but we know, and we are not able to act." Then he said: "The two rakʿahs you prayed are better than the world and all that is in it." Then he said: "May God reward the people of the world on our behalf with good. Convey to them the salutation, for there enters upon us from their supplication a light like the mountains."

Thus the purpose of visiting graves is, for the visitor, the admonition (iʿtibār) derived from them, and for the visited, the benefit of his supplication. The visitor should not neglect supplication for himself and for the deceased, nor neglect the admonition derived from him. Admonition is achieved by picturing in one's heart the deceased — how his parts have scattered, how he will be raised from his grave, and that he will soon be joined by [the visitor] himself.

As related from Muṭarrif ibn Abī Bakr al-Hudhalī: He said: There was an old woman among ʿAbd al-Qays, devoted to worship. When night came, she would gird herself, then stand at her prayer niche (miḥrāb); and when day came, she would go out to the graves. It reached me that she was reproached for her frequent coming to the cemeteries, and she said: "When the hard heart becomes dry, nothing softens it but the traces of decay (rusūm al-balā). I come to the graves, and it is as though I see them having emerged from between their layers; and as though I see those faces covered with dust, those bodies transformed, those eyelids oozing with grease. What a sight it is! If the servants were to drink it into their hearts, its bitterness would not deter their souls, nor its severity waste their bodies."

Rather, one should summon the image of the deceased as mentioned by ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, when a jurist entered upon him and was astonished at the change in his appearance due to the frequency of jihad and worship. He said to him: "O so-and-so, if you saw me after three [days], when I have been placed in my grave, and the two eyeballs have protruded and flowed down upon the cheeks, and the lips have shriveled back from the teeth, and pus has issued from the mouth, and the mouth has gaped open, and the belly has swollen and risen above the chest, and the spine has come out from the anus, and worms and pus have issued from the nostrils — you would see something more astonishing than what you now see."

It is commendable to speak well of the deceased and to mention him only with what is beautiful. ʿĀʾishah, may God be pleased with her, said: The Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, said: "When your companion dies, leave him and do not revile him." {«إذا مات صاحبكم فدعوه ولا تقعوا فيه»}

And he, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, said: "Do not revile the dead, for they have gone on to what they have sent forth." {«لا تسبوا الأموات فإنهم قد أمضوا إلى ما قدموا»}

And he, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, said: "Do not mention your dead except with good; for if they are among the people of Paradise, you incur sin [by speaking ill]; and if they are among the people of the Fire, then sufficient for them is what they are in." {«لا تذكروا موتاكم إلا بخير فإنهم إن يكونوا من أهل الجنة تأثموا وإن يكونوا من أهل النار فحسبهم ما هم فيه»}

Anas ibn Mālik said: A funeral procession passed by the Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, and they spoke well of it, and he, peace be upon him, said: "It is obligatory." Then another passed by and they spoke ill of it, and the Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, said: "It is obligatory." ʿUmar asked him about that, and he said: "This one — you spoke well of him, so Paradise is obligatory for him; and this one — you spoke ill of him, so the Fire is obligatory for him. And you are the witnesses of God upon the earth." {«إن هذا أثنيتم عليه خيرا فوجبت له الجنة وهذا أثنيتم عليه شرا فوجبت له النار وأنتم شهداء الله في الأرض»}

Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of God, may God's peace and blessings be upon him, said: "A servant may die, and the people praise him with praise while God knows other than that of him; then God, exalted be He, says to His angels: 'I make you witnesses that I have accepted the testimony of My servants concerning My servant, and I have overlooked what I know concerning My servant.'" {«إن العبد ليموت فيثني عليه القوم الثناء يعلم الله منه غيره ليقول الله تعالى لملائكته أشهدكم أني قد قبلت شهادة عبيدي على عبدي وتجاوزت عن علمي في عبدي»}

I say: Know that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said, as reported by Abū Hurayrah: "When the servant dies, two black-and-blue angels come to him; one is called Munkar and the other Nakīr. They say: 'What did you used to say about the Prophet?' If he was a believer, he says: 'He is the servant of Allah and His Messenger. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and that Muḥammad is His Messenger.' They say: 'We knew indeed that you would say this.' Then his grave is widened for him seventy cubits by seventy cubits, and it is illuminated for him. Then it is said to him: 'Sleep.' He says: 'Let me return to my family to inform them.' They say: 'Sleep.' So he sleeps like the sleep of a bride—none awakens him except the most beloved of his family to him—until Allah raises him from that resting place. But if he was a hypocrite, he says: 'I do not know; I heard the people saying something, and I said it.' They say: 'We knew indeed that you would say this.' Then it is said to the earth: 'Close in upon him.' So it closes in upon him until his ribs interlock; he remains tormented until Allah raises him from that resting place." {PageV04P502}

And it is reported from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him): "O ʿUmar, how will you fare when you die, and your people take you away, and they measure for you three cubits by a cubit and a span, then they return to you, wash you, shroud you, embalm you, then carry you until they place you in it, then they pour the dust over you and bury you? When they depart from you, the two examiners of the grave—Munkar and Nakīr—come to you; their voices are like crashing thunder, their glances like flashing lightning; they drag their hair and dig into the grave with their fangs; they jostle you and shake you. How will you fare at that moment, O ʿUmar?" ʿUmar said: "Will my intellect remain with me as it is now?" He said: "Yes." He said: "Then I shall suffice against them both."

This is an explicit text that the intellect does not change with death; rather, only the body and limbs change. Thus the deceased remains rational, perceptive, and cognizant of pains and pleasures just as before—nothing of his intellect alters. The intellect that perceives is not these limbs; rather, it is an inner thing having neither length nor breadth. Indeed, that which is indivisible in itself is the perceiver of things. Even if all the limbs of a person were to disintegrate, leaving only the perceiving part that is neither partitioned nor divided, the rational human being would remain complete and enduring. And so it is after death, for that part is not dissolved by death, nor does non-existence befall it.

Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir said: "It has reached me that upon the disbeliever in his grave is set a blind, deaf beast, in whose hand is a whip of iron, at whose head is something like the hump of a camel; it strikes him with it until the Day of Resurrection. It does not see him to avoid him, nor hear his voice to have mercy on him."

Abū Hurayrah said: "When the deceased is placed in his grave, his righteous deeds come and surround him. If it comes to him from the direction of his head, his recitation of the Qurʾān comes; if from his feet, his standing in prayer comes; if from his hand, the two hands say: 'By Allah, he used to extend me for charity and supplication—you have no way against him.' And if it comes from his side, his remembrance and fasting come. Likewise, prayer and patience stand aside, and it says: 'As for me, had I seen any breach, I would have been its mender.'" Sufyān said: "His righteous deeds shield him just as a man shields his brother, family, and children." Then it is said to him at that point: "May Allah bless you in your resting place; excellent are the companions, your companions; excellent are the associates, your associates."

And it is reported from Ḥudhayfah: "We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) at a funeral. He sat at the head of the grave, then began looking into it, then said: 'The believer is squeezed in this grave with a squeeze that causes his sinews to be displaced.'" {PageV04P503}

ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: "The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'The grave has a squeeze; had anyone been saved or escaped from it, it would have been Saʿd ibn Muʿādh.'" (Reported by Aḥmad.)

And it is reported from Anas: "Zaynab, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), died, and she was a sickly woman. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) followed her bier, and his state distressed us. When we reached the grave and he entered it, his face turned yellow with pallor. When he came out, his face brightened. We said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we saw from you a strange state—what was that?' He said: 'I recalled the squeeze of my daughter and the severity of the torment of the grave. I came and was informed that Allah has lightened it for her. Yet she was squeezed with a squeeze whose sound was heard between the East and the West.'" {PageV04P503}

